      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While a heartfelt manifesto can be a powerful tool for reclaiming your legacy, framing your work as \"stolen\" rather than \"influential\" often fuels more backlash than it generates genuine cultural truth in the current digital landscape. Instead of focusing on legal concepts of plagiarism, consider drafting a piece that highlights the vulnerability in your original releases three years ago—such as testing unreleased tracks on close friends or beta launch communities—and how algorithms now isolate those specific sonic signatures without understanding the context. This approach shifts the narrative from a fight for ownership to a critique of how data and capital have commodified artistic intimacy, which may resonate more deeply with listeners and allies within the scene. Remember that true influence means letting the work's evolution make itself known, even when the personal systems protecting it fail, so your legacy might endure by simply proving your vision continues while the trend fades.",
